HOW THIS PROJECT IS LICENSED, IN THREE PARTS
================================================================================

Whity is deliberately split so that plugin authors are never bound by the
licence of the host. Which licence applies depends on which part you touch:

  AGPL-3.0-only        Whity Core itself -- src/, public/, database/,
  (+ plugin exception) web/, and everything not listed below.
                       Modify it and serve it over a network, and you owe your
                       users the source.

  MIT                  The plugin boundary, both halves:
                         - whity/plugin-sdk        (PHP: interfaces, HTTP
                                                    shapes, event constants)
                         - @amroksaleh/ui          (React component library)
                         - @amroksaleh/features    (feature modules)
                         - @amroksaleh/api-client  (typed API client)
                         - @amroksaleh/tokens      (design tokens)
                       Build against these and your plugin is yours: license
                       it however you like, including commercially.

  Your choice          Plugins that depend only on the packages above. See the
                       WHITY PLUGIN EXCEPTION in LICENSE.

================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY DEPENDENCIES
================================================================================

Whity Core depends on the following third-party software. None of it is
redistributed in this source repository -- `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are
not committed -- but published container images do bundle it, which is why it
is attributed here.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHP (composer)  -- 10 packages, all permissive
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  MIT
    composer/semver
    defuse/php-encryption
    paragonie/constant_time_encoding
    paragonie/random_compat
    psr/clock
    psr/log
    spomky-labs/otphp
    symfony/deprecation-contracts
    whity/plugin-sdk

  BSD-3-Clause
    firebase/php-jwt

No PHP dependency carries a copyleft licence. Adding one would be a decision
with consequences for the whole distribution, not a routine bump.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
JavaScript (npm)  -- 1,379 packages with a declared licence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

By licence:

    1126  MIT
      90  ISC
      47  Apache-2.0
      25  MPL-2.0
      21  BSD-3-Clause
      14  BSD-2-Clause
      14  BlueOak-1.0.0
      13  Apache-2.0 OR MIT
      10  LGPL-3.0-or-later
       ... and a small tail of other permissive licences

The copyleft-family dependencies, and why each is compatible:

  LGPL-3.0-or-later -- @img/sharp-libvips-*
      Prebuilt libvips binaries behind `sharp`, which Next.js uses for image
      optimisation. LGPLv3 is explicitly compatible with AGPLv3, and these are
      separately-installed native binaries the user can replace, not code
      linked into Whity's own sources.

      WORTH KNOWING: an eventual proprietary commercial licence of Whity Core
      cannot simply absorb these. LGPL section 4 allows bundling in a
      proprietary work only where the user can replace the library. That is
      satisfied today because sharp is an ordinary npm dependency -- but it is
      a constraint to remember, not a settled matter to forget.

  MPL-2.0 -- lightningcss (and platform binaries), axe-core
      File-level copyleft and compatible with AGPL. Both are build- and
      test-time tools; neither ships in a deployed application.

